From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:35:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] The water probe is exempt from the water rule

Patch 0020 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Patch 0019 gave the shadows the user wanted and left the reflections still missing. The fault is
in that patch and is exact: the pass-through it adds for water lives in SurfaceAnyHit, and the
water probe uses that same hit group. So a rule written to stop the GATHERING rays halting at
water also stopped the one ray that has to see it. Water went invisible to everything.

The probe now marks its own payload with a negative coverage before tracing, and the any hit
honors the mark. The probe never reads that field back, nothing writes it during traversal, and
no real coverage is ever negative, so it cannot be mistaken for one.

The shadows from 0019 are deliberately unchanged. The user settled that question while this was
being written: "honestly i dont even feel like water needs to have surface shadow. iot looks odd
because the lack of reflection".

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -287,7 +287,15 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
         water.hitNormal = 0.0f;
         water.shadedColor = 0.0f;
         water.hitVelocity = 0.0f;
-        water.coverage = 1.0f;
+        // THE PROBE MARKS ITSELF (patch 0020). The water pass-through added for the foliage work
+        // lives in SurfaceAnyHit, and this probe uses that same hit group, so the rule meant to
+        // stop the GATHERING rays halting at water also stopped this one seeing it, which is the
+        // single ray that has to. Water went invisible to everything and the reflections with it.
+        //
+        // A negative coverage is the mark. The probe never reads the field back (it takes the
+        // normal and the distance), nothing writes it during traversal, and no real coverage is
+        // ever below zero, so it cannot be confused with one.
+        water.coverage = -1.0f;
         // REFLECTION CANDIDATE B (patch 0018, rungs 28 and 29): the PROBE looks wider, rather than
         // any instance's mask changing. Water carries the depth mask now that the foliage rule
         // classifies it as solid, so a probe that also traces depth finds it again, and no tree's
@@ -1554,7 +1562,8 @@ void SurfaceAnyHit(inout SurfacePayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionA
     // classified. A horizontal face of a depthless translucent draw is passed through exactly as
     // it was before the foliage work, so the primary reaches the bed, the shadow lands on the bed,
     // and the probe finds the water in front of it. A vertical one is foliage and is unaffected.
-    if (hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
+    if ((payload.coverage >= 0.0f) && hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) &&
+        (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
         IgnoreHit();
         return;
     }
